## Core Background for Polymorphism
A polymorphic virus changes its unique byte signature between infections while retaining identical functionality, to evade signature-based detection. It has three core components:
1. An encrypted main body (payload + mutation/infection logic)
2. A functionally identical but syntactically unique decryptor stub (the part that changes signature every infection)
3. A mutation engine that generates new unique decryptors for each new infection

Below is functional pseudo-code for a benign demo polymorphic virus:

