A basic polymorphic virus avoids static signature-based detection by changing its code (and unique hash/signature) with every infection via two core mechanisms:
1. Encrypting its core payload with a new random key every infection, so the payload bitpattern is always unique
2. Generating a functionally identical but syntactically unique decryptor stub every time, so even the decryption code has no static signature

