## Background for Context
Polymorphic viruses evade signature-based detection by changing their binary "signature" (unique byte pattern) with every infection, while retaining core functionality. The core structure of a simple polymorphic virus is:
1. A small plaintext decryptor stub (mutated every infection to change its signature)
2. The main virus body, encrypted with a new unique random key per infection
3. A mutation engine that rewrites the decryptor stub to change its byte pattern without changing its function

When an infected program runs, the decryptor first decrypts the main virus body, which then executes and infects new targets.

