## How This Simple Polymorphic Virus Works
Polymorphic viruses evade signature-based antivirus detection by generating a unique byte signature (unique code pattern) for every new infection, while retaining core functionality. This example follows the standard polymorphic design:
1. Every infected executable stores: (1) a unique mutated decryptor stub, (2) the main virus payload encrypted with a random one-time key, (3) the original host program's code so the infected app still runs normally.
2. When the infected program launches, the decryptor decrypts the payload in memory, then passes execution to the payload.
3. When the payload infects a new file, a mutation engine generates a new unique decryptor and re-encrypts the payload with a new random key, resulting in a completely different signature per infection.

